# 9. SECRET SANTA MATCHING SYSTEM

The admin must be able to specify:

- Registration deadline.
- Matching date and time.
- Reveal date and time.
- Event timezone, default Asia/Kolkata.

These may be different.

Participants must be prevented from joining after registration closes unless the admin explicitly reopens registration.

### Matching requirements

- Matching happens securely on the backend.
- Matching must never be calculated or disclosed on the frontend before reveal.
- Every participating user sends a gift to exactly one other user.
- Every participant receives exactly one gift.
- Nobody can be assigned themselves.
- Matches must be random and unbiased among valid assignments.
- No duplicate recipients.
- No missing assignments.
- Handle participant counts of zero, one, two, and larger groups.
- Use a cryptographically secure randomness source.
- Persist the resulting matches.

Use a suitable unbiased derangement generation approach.

Do not implement a naïve random shuffle that can fail unpredictably or silently produce self-matches.

For a participant count of one, report that matching is impossible.

### Server-side matching

Create a protected Vercel Function for executing matching.

Use an authenticated scheduled trigger, such as Vercel Cron, to check matching eligibility.

Enforce the configured event timestamp on the server regardless of when the scheduled invocation runs.

Make matching idempotent and concurrency-safe.

Use a transactional event lock or equivalent concurrency control to ensure two requests cannot create different assignments.

Design the persistence mechanism to respect Firestore transaction and batch-operation limits.

If an event has already been matched, do not reshuffle assignments automatically.

Provide an explicit admin-only reset policy for test events.

After matching, participants should still see only the waiting screen until their reveal time.

